8. CENTRAL FINANCIAL CALCULATION AND LEDGER ENGINE
This is a critical subsystem. Implement it as a shared, independently testable domain service. Dashboards, analytics, risk, prop-firm rules, reports, and AI must consume the same verified calculations. Never implement separate formulas in individual pages.

At minimum, design and eventually implement calculations for:
- Gross P&L and net P&L.
- Realised and floating/unrealised P&L.
- P&L percentage and return on capital.
- Position size, quantity, contracts, and lot size.
- Pip/tick/point distance and value.
- Risk amount and risk percentage.
- Planned reward/risk, initial R, and achieved R.
- Weighted average entry and exit prices.
- Partial-exit allocation.
- Commission, swap, fees, spread, and slippage.
- Margin and leverage.
- Balance, equity, available margin, and exposure where source data allows.
- Drawdown, daily loss, weekly loss, monthly loss, and trailing drawdown.
- Expectancy, win rate, average win/loss, payoff ratio, and profit factor.
8.1 Financial accuracy requirements
1. Use decimal-safe arithmetic (a suitable decimal library or integer minor units where appropriate) for currency, prices, quantities, and fees. Avoid relying on binary floating-point arithmetic for money-critical calculations.
2. Use instrument-specific specifications: contract size, tick size, pip size, tick/pip value, quote currency, lot conventions, and asset-class rules. Do not assume every FX pair, gold symbol, index, crypto asset, or futures contract has identical specifications.
3. Support account base currency and instrument profit currency. Convert only using a known historical/current FX rate, a documented user-provided rate, or an explicitly selected source. If conversion data is missing, show the native-currency amount and mark account-currency conversion unavailable.
4. Keep gross P&L, net P&L, commissions, swaps, fees, and cash movements distinct and reconcile them consistently. Do not double-count transaction costs.
5. Distinguish trade P&L from deposits, withdrawals, transfers, payouts, and manual account adjustments.
6. Distinguish balance from equity. Do not show real-time floating P&L unless a connected/verified price source or user-entered mark exists.
7. Document the formula and convention used for each metric, including denominator, time zone, day boundary, treatment of breakeven trades, and treatment of fees.
8. Where data is incomplete, return an explicit unavailable/insufficient-data result, not a guessed value or zero.
9. Make rounding and display precision instrument- and currency-aware. Store sufficient precision and round only at appropriate boundaries.
10. Preserve original imported values and source identifiers for auditability.
8.2 Metric definitions and testability
Document consistent definitions for win rate, expectancy, average win, average loss, payoff ratio, profit factor, drawdown, recovery factor, R multiple, and return percentage. Make the treatment of breakeven trades configurable or clearly stated. Time-series metrics such as Sharpe and Sortino must use a documented return series and sampling/annualisation convention; do not calculate them from arbitrary per-trade values without explaining the method.

Create deterministic unit tests for long and short positions, multiple instruments, currency conversion, partial exits, multiple entries, fees, swaps, winning/losing/breakeven trades, open positions, missing inputs, zero/negative edge cases, and account reconciliation.
